{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-95b8d0395f872f50", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# BigVGAN: A Universal Neural Vocoder with Large-Scale Training\narXiv: 2206.04658\nAuthors: Sang-gil Lee, Wei Ping, Boris Ginsburg, Bryan Catanzaro, Sungroh Yoon\nYear: 2022 | Venue: \nTopic: vocoder\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of periodic activations into the generator to provide the desired inductive bias for audio synthesis.\n  2. Proposal of an anti-aliased multi-periodicity composition module that composes signal components with learnable periodicities and uses a low-pass filter to reduce high-frequency artifacts.\n  3. Successful scaling of the GAN vocoder up to 112M parameters by addressing large-scale training failure modes, such as early collapse and gradient explosion, without over-regularizing the generator or discriminator.\n  4. Demonstration of state-of-the-art zero-shot generation performance for various out-of-distribution scenarios, including unseen speakers, languages, recording environments, singing voices, and instrumental audio.\n\nTags: Vocoder, Vocoder/GAN\n===\n# High-Fidelity Audio Compression with Improved RVQGAN\narXiv: 2306.06546\nAuthors: Rithesh Kumar, Prem Seetharaman, Alejandro Luebs, Ishaan Kumar, Kundan Kumar\nYear: 2023 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We introduce Improved RVQGAN, a high-fidelity universal audio compression model that compresses 44.1 KHz audio into discrete codes at 8 kbps with minimal quality loss and fewer artifacts.\n  2. We identify and fix a critical codebook collapse issue in existing models that limits full bandwidth utilization by employing improved codebook learning techniques.\n  3. We identify that quantizer dropout degrades full-bandwidth audio quality and propose applying it with a probability of 0.5 to mitigate this side-effect while maintaining variable bitrate support.\n  4. We introduce impactful design changes to neural audio codecs, including periodic inductive biases via Snake activations, a multi-band multi-scale STFT discriminator, and a multi-scale mel reconstruction loss.\n  5. We demonstrate that our method is a universal audio compression model capable of handling speech, music, environmental sounds, varying sampling rates, and different audio encoding formats.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-RVQ, 压缩/语义声学-声学", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "aef0a7dc-5ba2-4099-ae4c-8dd5c3590816", "uuid": "1c3398b5-b892-42e4-b32f-38fe461fc1e6", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T06:49:46.007Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# BigVGAN: A Universal Neural Vocoder with Large-Scale Training\narXiv: 2206.04658\nAuthors: Sang-gil Lee, Wei Ping, Boris Ginsburg, Bryan Catanzaro, Sungroh Yoon\nYear: 2022 | Venue: \nTopic: vocoder\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of periodic activations into the generator to provide the desired inductive bias for audio synthesis.\n  2. Proposal of an anti-aliased multi-periodicity composition module that composes signal components with learnable periodicities and uses a low-pass filter to reduce high-frequency artifacts.\n  3. Successful scaling of the GAN vocoder up to 112M parameters by addressing large-scale training failure modes, such as early collapse and gradient explosion, without over-regularizing the generator or discriminator.\n  4. Demonstration of state-of-the-art zero-shot generation performance for various out-of-distribution scenarios, including unseen speakers, languages, recording environments, singing voices, and instrumental audio.\n\nTags: Vocoder, Vocoder/GAN\n===\n# High-Fidelity Audio Compression with Improved RVQGAN\narXiv: 2306.06546\nAuthors: Rithesh Kumar, Prem Seetharaman, Alejandro Luebs, Ishaan Kumar, Kundan Kumar\nYear: 2023 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We introduce Improved RVQGAN, a high-fidelity universal audio compression model that compresses 44.1 KHz audio into discrete codes at 8 kbps with minimal quality loss and fewer artifacts.\n  2. We identify and fix a critical codebook collapse issue in existing models that limits full bandwidth utilization by employing improved codebook learning techniques.\n  3. We identify that quantizer dropout degrades full-bandwidth audio quality and propose applying it with a probability of 0.5 to mitigate this side-effect while maintaining variable bitrate support.\n  4. We introduce impactful design changes to neural audio codecs, including periodic inductive biases via Snake activations, a multi-band multi-scale STFT discriminator, and a multi-scale mel reconstruction loss.\n  5. We demonstrate that our method is a universal audio compression model capable of handling speech, music, environmental sounds, varying sampling rates, and different audio encoding formats.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-RVQ, 压缩/语义声学-声学", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
